# Synopsis

Manage the tool set of the current session, or explore the tool tree and
tool documents.

# Operations

- "enable": add tools matching "pattern" to the session's extra tool list.
  Requires "pattern".
- "disable": remove tools matching "pattern" from the session's extra tool
  list. Requires "pattern".
- "list": list tools. When "enabled" is true, lists the tools currently
  available to the model (the default tool list plus the enabled extra
  tools); otherwise lists the tools matching "pattern" (all tools when
  "pattern" is omitted). "with_summary" adds each tool's short summary;
  "start_index" and "count" select a page of the result.
- "doc": read documents of a tool. Requires "toolname"; "docpaths" selects
  the documents to read (defaults to "README.md"; several documents can be
  read in one call). Documents that do not exist are silently omitted
  from the result.
- "listdoc": list the documents of a tool. Requires "toolname"; "pattern"
  can be used to filter by document path globs.

# Patterns

"pattern" is a list of space separated glob patterns. The matching target
depends on the operation: for enable and disable it matches tool names
(e.g. "manage_*", "shell"); for list, patterns without "/" match tool
names while patterns containing "/" match paths in the tool tree (e.g.
"builtin/*"), for listing items under the root of the tool tree, prefix
your pattern with a "/" (e.g. "/*"); for listdoc it matches document
pathnames under the tool's doc directory. A word starting with "@" names
a tool preset (a named list of patterns, e.g. "@default"); it is
expanded in place and can be mixed with other words.

# Tool tree

The tool tree is a classification tree over the available tools: directories
are categories and files are concrete tools (named after the tools). The
same tool may appear under multiple categories; each position is listed
separately.

# Tool documents

Tool documents are plain-text (usually Markdown) files under the tool's
doc directory; they hold extended reference material for the model.
"README.md" is the conventional entry point.

# Approval

"enable" and "disable" are automatically approved by default.

# Returns

A JSON object. On success: enable returns {"added_tools":[...],"count":...};
disable returns {"removed_tools":[...],"count":...}; list returns
{"list":[{"name" or "path":..., "summary"?:...},...],"total":...,
"start_index":...,"count":...}; doc returns
{"toolname":...,"docs":[{"docpath":...,"content":...},...]}; listdoc returns
{"toolname":...,"doclist":[...]}.
On failure: {"error": <reason>}; when rejected: {"rejected": <reason>}.

# Notes

- "enable" and "disable" only affect the session's extra tool list; the
  default tool list specified at session start is not affected.
- Enabling an already enabled tool, or disabling one that is not enabled,
  is a no-op: the result is an empty list, not an error.
- Enabling a tool only makes it available to the model; the tool is not
  executed until it is actually called (calls keep their own approval).
- Enabled tools are scoped to the current session.
- A tool may still be invisible to the model after being enabled if the
  model provider does not support it.
- Use this tool to inspect what is available and to adjust the session
  tool set; use the shell tool to read other files.
